{
  "evidence": "TEXT_C: Bill of Rights guaranteeing housing, education, healthcare, decent work and a minimum wage; rebuild the NHS as an integrated public service with added funding and public health measures; raise Child Benefit and state pensions, restore benefits for 16–17s, end means testing for residential care; adopt the EU Social Chapter and a negotiated minimum wage; fairer personal taxation (15p starter rate, widen basic band, remove NI ceiling for high earners, cut VAT on domestic fuel); protect and build social housing; free nursery education and restore student grants. TEXT_F: Reject the Social Chapter and a national minimum wage; keep taxes low (target 20p basic rate, cap top at 40p) and cut regulation; welfare into work with firm obligations; expand grammar/specialist schools and testing; privatise/marketise public services (rail, London Underground via PFI, Parcelforce, NATS); more police, mandatory minimum sentences, end automatic early release; maintain a credible nuclear deterrent and oppose devolution.",
  "reasoning": "TEXT_C prioritises care and equality through expanded universal services, social transfers, workers’ rights, and progressive taxation, matching the profile’s strongest foundations. TEXT_F emphasises proportionality, authority, and market competition—tax cuts, rejection of minimum wage and EU social protections, welfare conditionality, and tougher policing—so it aligns less with a care/equality‑heavy profile.",
  "confidence": "high",
  "winner": "TEXT_C"
}
